it runs every 0 and 5mins, 5AM and 6AM, on 4th and 5th of every month
#can we use commands using comma
No, commas are strictly used within the time fields to specify multiple execution times (for example, 0 9,12,15 * * * will run a job at 9 AM, 12 PM, and 3 PM). 

If you use a comma in the command section cause an error

To run multiple commands in a single crontab entry, you should use standard shell operators instead:
1. Semicolon (;) – Run sequentially, regardless of success
0 2 * * * /path/to/command1; /path/to/command2

2. Double Ampersand (&&) – Run only if the previous command succeeds
0 2 * * * /path/to/command1 && /path/to/command2

3. Double Pipe (||) – Run only if the previous command fails
0 2 * * * /path/to/command1 || /path/to/error-handling-command

conflict in day of the month and day of the week, usually cron treat these feils as OR 

In standard cron scheduling, fields usually act as an "AND" condition. For example, 0 5 * 2 * means "run at 5:00 AM AND only in February."

However, the Day of the Month (DOM) and Day of the Week (DOW) fields are the only exception to this rule. When you restrict both fields, they create a logical conflict, and cron resolves it by switching from an "AND" condition to an "OR" condition.

0 0 13 * 5 /path/to/command

this script will run every month 13 and also every week Friday, because cron treats it under OR 

cron sees 13 if the date is 13th it will execute and it will check (Day of week) if it is not 13th first one(day of month) fails and try to execute second one(Day of week) which 5, if that day is friday it will execute


How to force an "AND" condition (e.g., Friday the 13th)

if you want to execute the script on every month 13th and 13th should be friday, then only execute; use

(meaning it must be both the date and the weekday), You do this by keeping the DOW as a wildcard in cron, and using a shell test inside the command field to check the weekday:

0 0 13 * * [ $(date +\%u) -eq 5 ] && /path/to/command

[ $(date +\%u) -eq 5 ] act as command
/path/to/command act as command
date +%u returns the day of the week as a number from 1 to 7

[ $(date +\%u) -eq 5 ] ;if day of the week is friday then exit status 0, it will execute /path/to/command


